    880	| §P.P3 polylog approximation of pi(x) with named-exponent error, slot 4 — do non-symmetric / position-correlated ("paired") kernel weights w_{2j-1} != w_{2j} (or paired-resolution / boundary-only / antipair perturbations) beat hard cutoff for sigma_eff at fixed K_compute? Resolves the only remaining kernel-axis falsifier of slot-1 named-exponent corollary not addressed by S196 (log-Gaussian) or S242 (symmetric compactly-supported) | EXPERIMENT (negative-shape closure of the paired-kernel-axis falsifier; empirical L2-optimality of hard cutoff extends from symmetric (S242) to non-symmetric / position-correlated kernel families) | E (E1.5 + S195 + S196 + S202 + S240 + S241 + S242) — confirms hard cutoff is L2-optimal across ALL kernel families tested; F_GUE = 0.55 ± 0.06 stable across 17 kernels (8 symmetric + 7 paired + hard) | Thread 7 commit step 4/5 (S243). Built `experiments/analytic/polylog_approx_pi/paired_kernels.py` (~430 lines): per-sample cumulative loop computing c_k = 2 Re R(x^{rho_k}) for k=1..K_max=4000, then numpy-dot post-processing for each (kernel, K_compute) — 7 paired families + hard baseline, 4 K-targets, 20 paired samples, 3 anchors x in {10^7, 10^8, 10^9}. Built-in paired-sign-test post-processor. Kernels: hard (w_k=1), paired_hann (w_k = hann at pair resolution, w_{2j-1}=w_{2j}=hann((j-1)/(K/2-1))), paired_triangle (triangle at pair resolution), paired_riesz (Riesz at pair resolution), antipair_03 (w_{2j-1}=1.3, w_{2j}=0.7), antipair_05 (w_{2j-1}=1.5, w_{2j}=0.5), half_int (w_k=1 for k<K, w_K=0.5 — half-integer cutoff), boundary_pair (w_k=1 for k<K-1, w_{K-1}=0.75, w_K=0.25 — taper applied only to boundary pair). **Headline grid (96 summary cells, 1920 (anchor, x, K, kernel) per-sample triples)**: ZERO (anchor, K, kernel) cells where sigma_eff(kernel) < sigma_eff(hard) at paired sign-test p < 0.05 (one-sided). Smallest p_kernel_beats_hard across all 84 cells = 0.2517 (boundary_pair, 10^7 K=500, wins=12/20 — within 1-sigma noise of break-even). Mean sigma_eff/sigma_eff(hard) across 12 (anchor, K) cells, by kernel: boundary_pair=0.999, half_int=0.999, paired_riesz=1.12, paired_triangle=1.23, paired_hann=1.23, antipair_03=2.12, antipair_05=3.24. **Decisive HARD wins** at 17 cells with p_hard_beats_kernel < 0.05: antipair_05 in 7+ cells with p<0.01 (catastrophic at K=4000, 10^9: sigma_eff = 240 vs hard 47.5, ratio 5.05×); antipair_03 at 10^9 K>=1000 with p ≈ 0.001-0.002; paired_hann/triangle at 10^9 K=1000 + 10^8 K>=2000 with p in 0.006-0.021. **Boundary-only kernels (half_int, boundary_pair) are statistically indistinguishable from hard**: max |Delta(sigma_eff/sigma_eff_hard)| = 0.6%, all 24 cells within 1-sigma noise of break-even. The head-term penalty (1-w_K)^2 Var_K = 0.25 Var_K for half_int (resp. 0.625 Var for boundary_pair) is one zero's worth of variance, swamped by the tail Sum_{j>K} Var_j ≈ K Var_K log^2 K — half a zero's variance is ~1% of total variance, below detection at N=20. **Antipair scaling matches random-phase L2 prediction exactly**: Var(antipair) - Var(hard) = (1-w)^2 K Var_avg with (1-w_k)^2 = 0.09 for antipair_03 and 0.25 for antipair_05; observed sigma_eff^2 / sigma_eff_hard^2 ratios at K=4000 are 4.5 (antipair_03) and 25.5 (antipair_05), consistent with adding 0.09 K v_avg / 0.25 K v_avg to the residual head term once K saturates the tail. **Closes the only remaining kernel-axis falsifier of the Thread 3 closure**: S196 closed log-Gaussian (multiplicative damping); S242 closed symmetric compactly-supported; S243 closes non-symmetric / paired / boundary-only / antipair. Together: HARD CUTOFF IS EMPIRICALLY L2-OPTIMAL ACROSS ALL TESTED KERNEL FAMILIES (17 kernels, 180 (anchor, K, kernel) cells across slots 3+4). **GUE pair-correlation is kernel-invariant at second-moment level**: F_GUE := sigma_eff^2 / sigma_pred^2 = 0.55 ± 0.06 across all 17 kernels (cv ≈ 11%). The L2-optimality of hard transfers verbatim to the GUE-corrected regime. **Implication for slot-1 named-exponent c